From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 23:03:54 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] mb/lenovo: Do not promise Kernel DMA Protection in the VT-d
 help

The help of Intel VT-d on the ThinkCentre M920x said it gives Kernel DMA
Protection. Windows turns that on only when the DMAR table has the
platform opt-in flag, which tells the OS that the firmware protected the
memory from DMA before it started; this firmware sets neither the flag
nor any protection before the OS. Say what VT-d gives (passthrough, the
OS's own DMA protection) and what it does not. The W541's help of the
untrusted ports option names the same condition next to TPM 2.0.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/mainboard/lenovo/haswell/cfr.c     | 5 +++--
 src/mainboard/lenovo/m720q_m920q/cfr.c | 5 ++++-
 2 files changed, 7 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/mainboard/lenovo/haswell/cfr.c b/src/mainboard/lenovo/haswell/cfr.c
index c4e8a95e33..c4e4bb2076 100644
--- a/src/mainboard/lenovo/haswell/cfr.c
+++ b/src/mainboard/lenovo/haswell/cfr.c
@@ -528,8 +528,9 @@ static const struct sm_object dma_untrusted_ports = SM_DECLARE_BOOL({
 			  "of their own and bounce buffers, against a card that\n"
 			  "reads or writes memory it should not. Some cards may be\n"
 			  "slower.\n"
-			  "Not Windows Kernel DMA Protection (that needs TPM 2.0),\n"
-			  "and no protection before the OS starts.\n"
+			  "Not Windows Kernel DMA Protection (that needs TPM 2.0\n"
+			  "and the memory protected before the OS starts), and no\n"
+			  "protection before the OS starts.\n"
 			  "Default: off. Shown with Intel VT-d on.",
 	.default_value	= false,
 }, WITH_DEP_VALUES(&vtd, true));
diff --git a/src/mainboard/lenovo/m720q_m920q/cfr.c b/src/mainboard/lenovo/m720q_m920q/cfr.c
index 5685d0f196..db7b6ea8d0 100644
--- a/src/mainboard/lenovo/m720q_m920q/cfr.c
+++ b/src/mainboard/lenovo/m720q_m920q/cfr.c
@@ -686,7 +686,10 @@ static const struct sm_object vtd = SM_DECLARE_BOOL({
 	.opt_name	= "vtd",
 	.ui_name	= "Intel VT-d",
 	.ui_helptext	= "The IOMMU, for device passthrough to virtual machines and\n"
-			  "DMA protection (intel_iommu, Kernel DMA Protection).\n"
+			  "DMA protection by the OS (intel_iommu on Linux).\n"
+			  "Not Windows Kernel DMA Protection, which also needs the\n"
+			  "memory protected before the OS starts: the firmware does\n"
+			  "not do that.\n"
 			  "Default: on.",
 	.default_value	= true,
 });
-- 
